#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25758b2a-af8a-5a90-81ab-acfe72d274c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260201} {:answer/id #uuid "66ae0255-9a5f-52f4-acb5-19660b53dbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260769} {:answer/id #uuid "904beab1-505f-5e48-a793-2b0d5eec535a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261129} {:answer/id #uuid "d53f3519-e121-50e5-9ddf-3dbe90831402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261752} {:answer/id #uuid "e7611642-0621-5c4d-9835-a60ea38e0d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ae0255-9a5f-52f4-acb5-19660b53dbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260769} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b4f3e47-9d1d-52ec-bb38-4c32ab7f1c29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8fd9f34-b5c0-5dde-8e43-965eb6f71e26" :question/math-academy-id "q-127206" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨y+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and  $(0,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/98/98d7da0c7cd1e1f1006c1db74ae1a1e1f198d9066df6585745ee36548f0b8d96.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+(-2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2xy.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd158a1-8e1c-5409-9f7d-cc713afefd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260308} {:answer/id #uuid "49e0677d-703c-5fd2-bba6-5d10e8428357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260512} {:answer/id #uuid "afbd580d-7551-55b1-a2ab-a54c76c17535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261427} {:answer/id #uuid "d49ca082-e6c8-5be2-9a1d-8f3250a52777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261749} {:answer/id #uuid "f489697f-1e0a-5833-b73d-49cd2c52c047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49e0677d-703c-5fd2-bba6-5d10e8428357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260512} :answer-field/id #uuid "fec87113-a1f7-5b61-8ed5-83eb792efa1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9d8ed81-5fd6-581e-8bd7-3dd3517c5bfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-127223" :question/problem "![](images/41/414611a90c2227ff163d40a4f6f560bc67c471fa0f35724e459cec280e4904f9.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4\\,i+2\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2t}\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/11/11376e6ef18b151a6dd1a02dd09f20b72bce3160c718f4095b4f08b23721ff1f.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t}{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{2t}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(4)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}\\right)-(2)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{t}-t){|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =(1-2)-(4-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef40240-5dea-5969-9bf5-aa4953cf23a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260016} {:answer/id #uuid "57f7d7af-cfe4-5280-876a-0df898cd965b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260625} {:answer/id #uuid "6e49d8ea-e28e-5c91-87e1-566eb88277b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260846} {:answer/id #uuid "da6bceb7-3212-5a3d-8999-7aab1e79ca62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261804} {:answer/id #uuid "f232b69c-3511-56ed-b069-32c42592bb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57f7d7af-cfe4-5280-876a-0df898cd965b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260625} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d0cb2f-2881-58f1-a7e4-d56c754b767d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1caebf8b-fbe2-5515-a0df-5bb50a3ced16" :question/math-academy-id "q-127227" :question/problem "![](images/85/85b4da8c0fa35867871b6254ba20f505b7596f2c97226adb2ed01339fe4cbedf.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=5{e}^{-x}\\,i+4\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,e],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/55/559bbc20aa7d47eaad47a8975f80c8efb91bd03bc816357bc6047675dc755c9b.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =5{e}^{-x}=5{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=\\frac{5}{t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\left(\\frac{5}{t}\\right)\\cdot (1)-(4)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4a4e6e-8fd1-5522-991a-091f0cb886bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{y}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260102} {:answer/id #uuid "20290a0e-d346-5c30-942c-4f76e1fbd4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{y}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260151} {:answer/id #uuid "a3837ae4-763a-595f-890a-8cc4ef3801c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261299} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b24ec3-56ed-5096-95a0-c3abce767ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261495} {:answer/id #uuid "fa68bf59-8089-510b-855b-d18f12a481e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{y}^{2}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3837ae4-763a-595f-890a-8cc4ef3801c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261299} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e39b9c2-8ba1-5d6a-8730-4f02deaa62b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64b95036-f7c8-5305-8593-d176862393c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-127229" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=3x{y}^{3}\\,i+6x{y}^{4}\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa98f3db31860a463d0a551d5297895e61a6c041d852c04de04c44a194510961.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{4}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =24x{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{y}^{3}+24x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1844ee7-71f8-5f82-b00f-48f60255736b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}{\\int }_{-3}^{1}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261279} {:answer/id #uuid "af686067-c1f0-550a-9035-c1db108598e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261423} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f7ac06-c16a-56ad-828e-96b57398b8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}(2xy+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261505} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e73e44-365c-517b-be69-1b06c081287f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}{\\int }_{-3}^{1}(5{x}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261576} {:answer/id #uuid "e07c2725-c3eb-5417-b03a-c1bf16f00f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}(x-5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af686067-c1f0-550a-9035-c1db108598e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261423} :answer-field/id #uuid "59ffff1c-063b-5515-bce4-0632652300f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25b638ef-851a-5e05-bca0-4d63723c7d75" :question/math-academy-id "q-127231" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-3,-1),(-1,-1),(-1,1),$ and $(-3,1),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=(y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}x)\\,i+(-{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4})\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/54/5497f23e51d1e745fef1cf39d6c1a8ada22b25e8c9c7b87f5d5338e85a941739.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}x$ and $Q(x,y)=-{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2yx+{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2yx+4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2yx+{y}^{3})+(-2yx+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b4f1bb5-5af8-54dc-b770-fd3f05a8066c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260522} {:answer/id #uuid "5d592e34-b162-5fde-8afc-943efe3e8ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260678} {:answer/id #uuid "ad49cef3-b98d-5033-a436-5ffab9049391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261392} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb8232e-688e-5fce-98f8-d1fc7d49d436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261415} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb10982-6a16-5384-8d48-742898dfa1f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b4f1bb5-5af8-54dc-b770-fd3f05a8066c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260522} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9c8acf-0dab-51c5-ade8-bb82d29c0b7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7df129a-7685-5a03-8c7b-340092011e9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127233" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9bb15934c271a17851342b9032f5096f48e7ac9ee4e149af92ac1010dea46d88.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=3xy\\,i+(2x+1)\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/0e/0e751adb9f44d24ba89af318fe5f063b1be146bbae45d05fc2dd9a99bef259f2.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3xy=3{t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2x+1=2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2})(1)-(2t+1)(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}-2t-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-1-1)-(0-0-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07034c2e-8af8-57f8-a980-3075761e8455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259942} {:answer/id #uuid "137e64d0-7b45-5b0d-b600-d6bc9ea46c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260047} {:answer/id #uuid "639ba164-e39e-53eb-a66d-858b18447c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260735} {:answer/id #uuid "99c2bd41-610f-5d52-9a7d-8d782bd6205a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261206} {:answer/id #uuid "d74d9236-a4bb-589b-961a-53f185bb7d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "137e64d0-7b45-5b0d-b600-d6bc9ea46c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260047} :answer-field/id #uuid "275aecf1-178a-5df7-a9f4-4a5476f8a4ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b74be94c-a6f2-5d0a-8660-699d2ff2d45b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127244" :question/problem "![](images/60/6043a1f88474e2c282938d3b3f0193002b6e11b5fb6d16292fea43d63292d1b0.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y={x}^{3}$ from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=-3{x}^{2}\\,i+\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/1d/1d54fe3c07f04a7d05567942e3eea6efaf1cb516070a5d7cc31ef726c0ddb423.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-3{x}^{2}=-3{t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3{t}^{2})\\cdot (3{t}^{2})-(1)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-9{t}^{4}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9}{5}-1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14506f23-b7d1-50e2-b7d6-575d362006d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260054} {:answer/id #uuid "c9aa7ce3-f49a-53f5-8930-c62b6d6c8760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261659} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd35a62-5758-5dfb-8a79-58eaad86d417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261954} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c6fb02-0c1a-52ca-8814-4034572247cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262025} {:answer/id #uuid "fde5bf23-2aa5-5f61-aed8-c77dfd32c840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5c6fb02-0c1a-52ca-8814-4034572247cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262025} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ffccb8a-a39d-5a7a-a89e-228e3ce4f2c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "42dae85e-af3c-52f4-a342-edbfb5509ac9" :question/math-academy-id "q-127253" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd21413d8eea5e2fec67eebe22967c846fd7916fd4fb0fbe5f8efe7ac9df6b1a.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=1-x$ from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\,i+\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=1-t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/e2/e288ed1095be61e4645fb63c22b7fd2cb605d34795c30557e84afb31b7ec8721.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =1, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1)\\cdot (-1)-(1)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2-0 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62235db6-575c-58cc-9ea4-bb528398093e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260721} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8d5f64-67d3-5dce-8551-9f910cdef724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260944} {:answer/id #uuid "dc023b88-e9da-5465-abd7-a53791e497db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261823} {:answer/id #uuid "edf8fb76-10ab-53d9-a7da-5af08b0f7758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261967} {:answer/id #uuid "faea5b08-5f3d-5e20-a75e-e18c259cc4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edf8fb76-10ab-53d9-a7da-5af08b0f7758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261967} :answer-field/id #uuid "afced7f6-385b-5b50-b3de-6f71c6df96dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6e7eff5f-8b84-5752-bf2b-9206857b0d37" :question/math-academy-id "q-127254" :question/problem "![](images/67/67dca8f0202d0c5d1041935a6aee7c4ebd0e3c6c449f2ee1a5ff24c1f381410c.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ from the point $(1,\\frac{1}{e})$ to the point $(0,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [\\frac{1}{e},1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(1,\\frac{1}{e})$ to the point $(0,1).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/08/08fc4561a322b690da7bd28a9e69a91c687256e8a902ec7690dda5e6a1c689a6.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =0, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2{y}^{2}=2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=-\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}(0)\\cdot (1)-(2{t}^{2})\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}){|}_{1/e}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f014674-85e2-5712-94fa-a1a4f4088850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260429} {:answer/id #uuid "540fd8b7-839b-5252-8a65-5ad6720e3081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260583} {:answer/id #uuid "69707d0e-2e50-52a2-a276-f84bd14a62a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260796} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb13e75-d527-5698-b332-2b8a249bc796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261849} {:answer/id #uuid "fc605dab-f873-5fb2-8c9e-b56b75167414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfb13e75-d527-5698-b332-2b8a249bc796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261849} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1824784-022c-5d45-b426-731053e70693" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db5d90ba-c5db-5d34-b26f-640edea7636c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127303" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x,\\,{x}^{5}-3y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35e378bca35f4b0822c713556065415d59b5c566ec8fcc8e3124256aac7b7f14.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{5}-3y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-(-1))\\,dy \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =-2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21de3cf7-4b55-5b4f-ab6a-375c5a7d25e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260167} {:answer/id #uuid "a2335fe5-8b47-5e4b-835b-e3b58b0f5ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261286} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1ad8f1-09f9-5de7-ba25-01fe8f576f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261507} {:answer/id #uuid "c26a51aa-0eb7-55e1-8247-693120014201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261590} {:answer/id #uuid "f5911c8b-0c20-5a8d-91de-79dbd76c58cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21de3cf7-4b55-5b4f-ab6a-375c5a7d25e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260167} :answer-field/id #uuid "80d4081e-14ed-54f2-ad21-1cd5742e88d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fb71543-2fec-5ce1-b3ae-7f7da8898b87" :question/math-academy-id "q-127308" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-3{x}^{3},\\,x{e}^{x}-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/2696cdadd3cb020cfbe343b9c372893ac18b63f3548ff71003b7328b19de39d5.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-3{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{e}^{x}-4x{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-9{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-12x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-9{x}^{2}-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-3{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(-3(1)^{3}-6(1)^{2}{y}^{2})-(-3(-1)^{3}-6(-1)^{2}{y}^{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6)\\,dy \\\\  & =-6y\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "409e352b-1dfe-5ff9-aedc-75328dd5c0e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260440} {:answer/id #uuid "4152d260-9884-52bb-8f86-bc9609db7be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260446} {:answer/id #uuid "42d0fbc3-b082-5c28-ad06-10f183c57616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260456} {:answer/id #uuid "66b1bfdb-4012-5b43-9ff0-5d28b334ab44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260770} {:answer/id #uuid "eca1aa3c-6ff5-5fcb-bf3c-315c4ea87127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{50}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42d0fbc3-b082-5c28-ad06-10f183c57616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260456} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f348574-a376-5e8c-90e1-01f7851d5439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d270431a-39a1-5ea3-a306-51d0cd90b607" :question/math-academy-id "q-127309" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y},\\,3{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6e80e3010ec7c40d3f59dbb1da4e77c985c889a720b6f954177d8ad83a01351.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12yx,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12yx+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}(12yx+6y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{y}^{2}x+3{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}+3x)\\,dx \\\\  & =(2{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =22-0 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdeb3565-33b8-5e33-8e62-a379ccee15a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261551} {:answer/id #uuid "d61001de-9748-5fd5-a503-22e24e85614b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261757} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b6bc8d-1963-572f-81c7-31d894805c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261765} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2e40f2-95b7-5d9f-a3c0-5505414cee4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261946} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfa1d83-7d56-5a32-8910-1d3f25e76d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d61001de-9748-5fd5-a503-22e24e85614b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261757} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee924e6b-2ed7-5385-812d-4d3eda3ed721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1199c00-6bec-5b54-8e65-8c9cf313406e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127316" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨x-\\sqrt{y},\\,y-\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/c4/c4dd4d03bdfcf6c3d597e2fdef8681e4f3316b3f276cb9967933b61b0dc90976.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x-\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y-\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ef51b5-bef5-548c-8f51-a79f305af85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259957} {:answer/id #uuid "80081f79-d17b-5546-ab52-2540982bd374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260986} {:answer/id #uuid "806a70e6-fd99-58c6-a425-5c06d2336cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260991} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1969e2-dc11-53e4-a52a-96db98443bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261977} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc96c67-0441-581c-839f-b9e98cb25a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef1969e2-dc11-53e4-a52a-96db98443bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261977} :answer-field/id #uuid "0261ce20-dc45-5b39-aeaf-012d29e230ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5702cf64-a04c-533d-bb19-d90253b29fec" :question/math-academy-id "q-127322" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\sqrt[3]{y}+x,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea6c25e40b6683d9ff4a4884dbbda708fd79dbd79d0b50730f9ad6e165d2eb58.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}+x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2{x}^{3}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-2(2)^{3}y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-16y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[2y-8{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)-8(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23fba3b8-2900-5090-8e9f-03e7367e09eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260188} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6f3730-903b-591c-86aa-1010845f9300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260293} {:answer/id #uuid "4758297b-9cdb-529a-852e-7fbfc833d22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260495} {:answer/id #uuid "51730068-71f9-59da-a522-e4eeca4b1d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260565} {:answer/id #uuid "e89161a2-7639-5667-9c16-f59bf41e64af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51730068-71f9-59da-a522-e4eeca4b1d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260565} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e5dc6e5-2148-538c-b14d-fe9cfe804e13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "235350dc-4663-567f-92d6-1215c91e7efd" :question/math-academy-id "q-127323" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}+2y,\\,-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,1),$ and $(-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/3a/3ad0a876fe801980a2c3724a639fc6c379db750635c0278a10a5094e2d64c34a.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)=-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2y\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2y}^{2y}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}-10x]_{-2y}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(24{y}^{2}-20y)-(24{y}^{2}+20y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-40y)\\,dy \\\\  & =-40{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-40(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2058c9-6db4-581f-a065-7bb1cf07c556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260123} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8b210e-ceef-5d7e-a20b-9a79b427549e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260260} {:answer/id #uuid "be97a3e0-af5b-55f6-8a9d-668875533062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261556} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa02f2a-4b5c-5f74-9d43-bc4fc65096f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261568} {:answer/id #uuid "cacc2a97-a4f6-538c-9ceb-91985ed7b814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa02f2a-4b5c-5f74-9d43-bc4fc65096f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261568} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d22bdec-d0fd-5742-b103-7ec2dae7bac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d93ec47e-8065-5376-b8dd-7b5786ea175a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127324" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,0),$ and $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/34/3497b3bbc834879026235f97468f5feb26e91a2b73a4de3da87597bf422ecd33.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-2xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{2}-{x}^{2}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(1-y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(1-y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-((-1)-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c19fbd3-eb95-539d-bf87-676882069ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260268} {:answer/id #uuid "6abfd50c-9e0e-5020-a336-2ba73402223b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260811} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffe60d9-b0ff-56b4-841a-a0c93b74cf46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261127} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bfec2e-6d9e-56cc-b6d7-29dec1dfb273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261471} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f4d10d-23f5-5225-a928-01986a2f242c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6abfd50c-9e0e-5020-a336-2ba73402223b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260811} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa559da7-dc21-5a7b-ab0a-f489b126cb88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0a994fc8-218e-56c0-8e85-b472da8df87e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127325" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,{x}^{3}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/006d80a7dbe4d03b78cc5fb1a642405e3fdfdd69ec9d80014ee8e0355ad1ae73.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y/2}^{y/2}(y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[x]_{-y/2}^{y/2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[\\frac{y}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right)]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{4}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "078e1d3d-154f-599f-804b-65cdf3bf5c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259946} {:answer/id #uuid "081265ef-7d64-5006-90b3-2f63d3ca542c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259951} {:answer/id #uuid "0cef64ee-d9a4-5b31-92b5-2570a40f936e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259997} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf4863c-f3c3-5b68-9c7b-cc9770e292c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{55}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261543} {:answer/id #uuid "cc412525-0ccb-5025-bb87-ba1d5b4af512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "081265ef-7d64-5006-90b3-2f63d3ca542c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259951} :answer-field/id #uuid "ace3ee59-34da-56d9-81d0-45569af6fe18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e551ea97-e0b9-53f0-8487-f26c11c3ea32" :question/math-academy-id "q-127365" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨30xy,\\,15{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y,$ $\\,x=-\\sqrt{y},$ and the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/24/24369439bc62cf06084a43d97dff0cee4e4089fd103511ef73d9dd5af1256ebb.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=30xy$ and $Q(x,y)=15{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=30y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =30y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(30y+30y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y}}^{y}60y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}y[x]_{-\\sqrt{y}}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}y(y+\\sqrt{y})\\,dy \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}+{y}^{3/2})\\,dy \\\\  & =60{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{2{y}^{5/2}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =60(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{5})-0 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "786fd2f0-0c3d-5b7a-b5bc-a3608fc69fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+9}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260929} {:answer/id #uuid "91d23bb2-32fb-5fbc-9842-9fb48c39267d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261143} {:answer/id #uuid "9501dc4d-d6ab-5f38-966b-90a4a703d054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+16}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261170} {:answer/id #uuid "dac1fd6a-eb18-58b7-911b-730ac1c804e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261807} {:answer/id #uuid "fe36bad1-4fa4-557e-a374-9f534fc494fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe36bad1-4fa4-557e-a374-9f534fc494fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262105} :answer-field/id #uuid "f53642b3-fa2e-5032-9340-8b9099d29395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1a4d542-8890-5129-a744-baf652c9fc9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127419" :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ from $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "214ab720-6ecf-5a54-bbca-66e11f886130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260158} {:answer/id #uuid "491c9a30-b37c-5794-9dfe-c72bf6a272ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{2{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260507} {:answer/id #uuid "741301d8-03d2-596d-99cb-dea017a15918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260889} {:answer/id #uuid "99f9d829-a8f9-5d96-a7b8-f240b15576e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{2{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261210} {:answer/id #uuid "9a64fc13-46c7-54cc-b859-06bfde01dc08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "214ab720-6ecf-5a54-bbca-66e11f886130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260158} :answer-field/id #uuid "83cdb749-1449-536c-beab-38d88342a23d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae63a631-2f65-575f-9860-3fcf3ba50584" :question/math-academy-id "q-127427" :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ from $t=-1$ to $t=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}+{cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "094b9b5d-90a5-52e6-ad22-1226b75da63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi }-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259958} {:answer/id #uuid "4077f389-0099-57f1-8340-852121cc2cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260439} {:answer/id #uuid "6c320932-603e-58a3-a9fc-86f6548e4a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260824} {:answer/id #uuid "87a3a0e9-f0fa-50c2-9617-655e6d9e094e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261056} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a2e11f-134c-5105-9358-e819e1684bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c320932-603e-58a3-a9fc-86f6548e4a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260824} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8e0ad00-4b7d-5048-9a17-3b79e4ef2994" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76702025-335e-5381-8404-f40fe3fd7dfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-127573" :question/problem "![](images/16/162c0d0310cbbb533c30af373820751fc6c5a447127958486571224d7f8ac07a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ from $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{t}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =|{e}^{t}|\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{2({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{e}^{t}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78dc759e-2781-5f7c-8fdf-32c8a7488616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260936} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e65376-d802-5c70-a9b5-c7a733c9a942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261331} {:answer/id #uuid "bb19c345-96c8-5417-8091-0779682fa6e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261521} {:answer/id #uuid "e51f0554-9934-5f77-b1d4-2f9e022ea1c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261892} {:answer/id #uuid "e740a674-cf61-5f78-af1b-0865ebe203d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78dc759e-2781-5f7c-8fdf-32c8a7488616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260936} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7443e3d-bb79-5f31-9bc0-f76639543945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ea8f2b8-d1da-5f6e-96c2-1f49a78fce12" :question/math-academy-id "q-127578" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -9 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -9 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} & =5\\cdot 7-(-9)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =35-27 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2350f7db-d0b1-580d-8303-5d5534a14ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260185} {:answer/id #uuid "2f380174-748c-5220-b6ab-bb1fd86d6300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260300} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa4f15b-ff78-536d-9c98-39423cde1291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260305} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc2802a-b9f4-555c-a30c-fdb056782082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260307} {:answer/id #uuid "62c209ca-c14b-5357-9099-3070ded3b153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f380174-748c-5220-b6ab-bb1fd86d6300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260300} :answer-field/id #uuid "83ca020d-e4a7-5b74-b14e-0f4db14e8f9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcfbc0ab-8a2a-5b55-a599-576a8e82d92e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127580" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & =-1\\cdot 5-(-10)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-5-20 \\\\  & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "645b7b78-a1b2-5fca-b90e-39d47899cd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260741} {:answer/id #uuid "88e591c2-1771-5bd4-9969-13cd8f27d0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261066} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f29c98-2b8a-5e10-afa8-61e4c808e5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261488} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d54c34-a83c-514e-812b-09d754b2eef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261632} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd06f32-fb58-584a-a752-9e7577253c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6d54c34-a83c-514e-812b-09d754b2eef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261632} :answer-field/id #uuid "df607fbe-708b-5c81-8093-49116a0267c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f15b24c-9b34-54f4-8404-a1b55cbcfc8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127585" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4aa91668440687bbae4b1f85672f5cbaf7ce00d978049c7558eed99b7361d1a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨3t-{t}^{3},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩$ from $t=-1$ to $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3-3{t}^{2},\\,6t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(3-3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(6t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9-18{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =3({t}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+1-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{3}+1+\\frac{1}{3}+1) \\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1729317d-f622-5dce-8c02-3f5018dcb8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260077} {:answer/id #uuid "86f360a8-158d-58e7-8648-0876bd49a4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3xy}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261052} {:answer/id #uuid "8a58d3cf-de39-5f9f-b770-d6d11f0da4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261081} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d927b0-5361-5416-bb9f-d86d1935176e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5xy}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261316} {:answer/id #uuid "ac2d9a29-e850-51a7-ad56-c6faf0894dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a58d3cf-de39-5f9f-b770-d6d11f0da4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261081} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c41b5de-ab83-554f-95bf-42d11f54be45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26e4f5d9-b2df-5b89-afc1-259e97c4e9b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-127601" :question/problem "Calculate $det(C)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}-x & -2y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 5y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-x & -2y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 5y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-x\\cdot 5y-(-2y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =-5xy+xy \\\\  & =-4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33362404-a630-58e0-ab60-b015a97a07bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260338} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9f6888-780f-56da-9b96-be6ea61dfe14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261094} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b610b3-0649-5a84-aede-f6eaefb07746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261437} {:answer/id #uuid "d2902a59-e5b1-5475-92d3-40644cd440c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261727} {:answer/id #uuid "d811aa28-4287-58e1-aa7e-780dedbda6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2902a59-e5b1-5475-92d3-40644cd440c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261727} :answer-field/id #uuid "a908bb4c-3596-56c3-974f-5d6999f2fa97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3244e29a-ed4a-595a-8cf3-8bd475ff4af0" :question/math-academy-id "q-127602" :question/problem "![](images/33/33412629fc30584cf4a1726eef7e4f23f623ae4439d25045d61e36f68914dacd.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\frac{1}{3}({t}^{2}+2)^{3/2}⟩$ from $t=-1$ to $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}({t}^{2}+2)^{3/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+t){|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}+2)-(\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}+2+\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5498ee4c-4372-5204-b6e1-f57f31f7ce72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260592} {:answer/id #uuid "61a3d195-a5ae-5921-ac90-3b4c24a6e5e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(2{x}^{2}+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260716} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad83484-0f5d-5184-b122-b899402359be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261219} {:answer/id #uuid "9f32a52b-bd39-5040-afc5-c0f0787ecc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261256} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a8698b-73f4-57ac-a21f-031d3c8b223a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(2{x}^{2}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61a3d195-a5ae-5921-ac90-3b4c24a6e5e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(2{x}^{2}+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260716} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1e2c382-0ce2-5adc-b135-69fb578a8e4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5adfe9df-8aee-5967-99f3-985aed9cc4da" :question/math-academy-id "q-127603" :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & y \\\\ -5 & xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & y \\\\ -5 & xy\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot xy-y\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y+5y \\\\  & =y(2{x}^{2}+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "231d65c4-afe9-5dda-9ec8-8e657ebc2e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260183} {:answer/id #uuid "2cde1f0f-ce43-5b48-8b5f-a4207965af8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260279} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9b7936-2a9f-5fa6-98b8-c2d31209e615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260296} {:answer/id #uuid "6e18de3f-d88f-51e5-8a5a-4c7a07f3003b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260842} {:answer/id #uuid "72f9541a-9495-523a-a73a-4a7b746e235a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cde1f0f-ce43-5b48-8b5f-a4207965af8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260279} :answer-field/id #uuid "469de0af-655e-5c52-99af-6a01835c14fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb9b1b8a-cede-5e44-9f81-ee8a71692716" :question/math-academy-id "q-127604" :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 2k & -k+2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-1$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 2k & -k+2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-k+2)-(-5)\\cdot 2k \\\\  & =7k+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(U)=-1$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =-1 \\\\ 7k+6 & =-1 \\\\ 7k & =-1-6 \\\\ 7k & =-7 \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "613ae31f-3ed4-522f-b38e-30b725565300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260710} {:answer/id #uuid "6c66b584-1346-57a8-91c6-b84e73a70782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260826} {:answer/id #uuid "8ccc1966-d22e-5ca2-85e6-62f888a4ebc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261106} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbbb63f-c7de-5798-b663-aa393eb8f406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261850} {:answer/id #uuid "f645c953-47f3-5b1c-9345-c05fb1f18f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c66b584-1346-57a8-91c6-b84e73a70782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260826} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ae8e73c-6de7-5bcc-a8b5-a2c66229f23e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bb355a7-6406-51ad-b4a4-0aaf09eca17d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127606" :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}v & 2v \\\\ 2 & v+8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(T)=-4$, what is the value of $v?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & 2v \\\\ 2 & v+8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot (v+8)-2v\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={v}^{2}+4v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(T)=-4$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =-4 \\\\ {v}^{2}+4v & =-4 \\\\ {v}^{2}+4v+4 & =0 \\\\ (v+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aab353e-d8a2-5cee-ad8e-75d89f36942f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259969} {:answer/id #uuid "55f69ea5-658f-54da-aab9-eba230208482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260610} {:answer/id #uuid "77bbba7e-5eef-5fde-bf0b-27cf27e274ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260919} {:answer/id #uuid "e35430a5-8bd9-5cd3-a709-f9d32c9a6cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261873} {:answer/id #uuid "f83c7a76-e478-5e97-87ce-06f9facc6cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77bbba7e-5eef-5fde-bf0b-27cf27e274ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260919} :answer-field/id #uuid "481e7819-63cc-5385-bd4b-6641777b91a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "612dd17c-57fb-5f36-b33a-8e40d39733fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-127607" :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle P=\\begin{bmatrix}r-3 & r \\\\ 3 & r\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(P)=-9$, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =\\begin{vmatrix}r-3 & r \\\\ 3 & r\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(r-3)\\cdot r-r\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={r}^{2}-6r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=-9$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =-9 \\\\ {r}^{2}-6r & =-9 \\\\ {r}^{2}-6r+9 & =0 \\\\ (r-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ r & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
